# docs/ is local working notes, except the files every clone needs. A note that
# becomes a rule moves into conventions.md rather than staying on one machine.
docs/*
!docs/conventions.md
!docs/style-guide-grid-app.md
!docs/architecture.md
!docs/panel-protocol.md
!docs/git-auto-install.md
.playwright-mcp/*

# ── The device firmware under device/ ────────────────────────────────────────
# An ESP-IDF build tree is tens of thousands of files: the previous firmware
# dragged 406 MB of build/, managed_components/ and a generated sdkconfig into
# this repo before anyone noticed. These four lines are why that cannot happen
# again, and they go in BEFORE the first `idf.py build`, not after.
#
# What IS tracked, deliberately:
#   partitions.csv        — dual OTA is a protocol fact (docs/panel-protocol.md
#                           §"Firmware update"), not a local build choice
#   sdkconfig.defaults    — the intent; sdkconfig is what a build generates from
#                           it, and only the intent is reviewable
#   dependencies.lock     — the exact managed component versions, so two
#                           machines build the same firmware
device/*/build/
device/*/managed_components/
device/*/sdkconfig
device/*/sdkconfig.old
device/*/sdkconfig.superseded
device/*/sdkconfig.defaults.stamp
# clangd's index, 8.5 MB of it and 1,331 files, written by whichever editor
# opened the firmware. It reads as source because it sits beside it.
device/*/.cache/

# THE FIRMWARE IMAGE IS TRACKED, and it was not until 2026-08-19. The rule here
# used to ignore it — "the repo keeps the recipe, not the loaf" — which is a good
# instinct and was the wrong call, because nothing in the release pipeline bakes
# the loaf: CI runs no ESP-IDF and copies no .bin, so every shipped DMG carried
# NO panel firmware at all.
#
# What that cost a user: plug a panel into a store-installed Grid, and if the two
# disagree about the protocol the panel shows "Panel needs an update — Grid can
# reflash it over this cable" and greets forever. The app cannot: it has nothing
# to offer, returns null from panelFirmwareProvider and never says so on screen.
# A panel bought with the app was a brick the app promised to fix.
#
# The cost of tracking it is real and smaller: ~2.4 MB per firmware bump in
# history. Building it in CI is the better answer and the one that ends this rule
# (it needs ESP-IDF in the runner); until then the loaf ships with the recipe.
#
# assets/panel/ itself must keep existing: pubspec.yaml declares it and Flutter
# fails the build outright on a declared asset directory that is absent.
